{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Jacob went out from Be'er-sheva and walked toward Haran.</b> Rashi explained: there was no need to write anything but 'and Jacob walked toward Haran'; rather, it informs us that the departure of a righteous person from a place makes an impression. Now one must wonder: did Abraham and Isaac not go from place to place? And why was the language of 'going out' not written concerning them? And there are two sides to this, for according to the initial reasoning, the reason he employed 'went out' here is precisely because of the greater distinction: Abraham and Isaac did not leave behind in the place from which they went a righteous person like themselves — it is obvious that their departure made an impression. But here, behold, Isaac and Rebecca remained there, so you might think that his departure did not make an impression — therefore it informs us that it did. And a parallel to this is brought in Bereshit Rabbati (Genesis Rabbah 68:6), which brings a proof from 'and she went out from the place' (Ruth 1:7) said concerning Ruth, and asks: there it is fine, for there was only that one righteous woman alone there; but here, where Isaac and Rebecca were present? Rabbi Azariah says: the merit of one righteous person is not comparable to the merit of two righteous persons — thus far his language." "And one may explain from another side, to say that precisely Jacob's departure made an impression, but not the departure of Abraham and Isaac, because they went with all the members of their household and no righteous person remained in the place; therefore their departure did not make an impression, for the wicked who remained there are not particular about the departure of the righteous — and on the contrary, they rejoice when the righteous leave them, because the wicked do not walk in their ways, and the righteous are a thorn in their eyes and a prickle in their sides, and they quarrel with them on account of their deeds. But one who leaves a place where righteous people are present — then his departure makes an impression, for the righteous who remain find their separation difficult, for their minds are at ease because of him, and the merit of few is not comparable to the merit of many. And here Isaac and Rebecca were present, therefore the impression of his departure was noticeable. And according to this, we must say that even in the field of Moab there were pious ones of the nations of the world." "Another matter: the reason the language of 'going out' is mentioned here, while in another place (Genesis 12:10) it says 'and Abram went down to Egypt,' is because the land of Israel is the place of the revelation of His presence, blessed be He, and one who goes from there — it is a descent for him and a departure from the line of equality, for every 'going out' is from a place where one properly belongs, as in 'I go out in the midst of Egypt' (Exodus 11:4). It turns out that this departure made an impression even upon him himself. And in the Yalkut (Genesis 28, remez 117) it concludes: 'Sometimes a person's mate comes to him, and sometimes he goes to his mate. Jacob went to his mate, as it is said, \"And Jacob went out from Be'er-sheva.\"' And how does he learn from this verse that he went to his mate when he fled from Esau? This is nothing but the difficulty of 'And Jacob went out' — it should have said 'and he walked'! This compelled him to say that Jacob removed his thought entirely and completely from the dwelling-place of his father and mother, therefore he is called 'one who goes out,' to tell you that he departed completely. To distinguish from one who leaves a certain place intending to return — then his thought always wanders in the place from which he went, and such a one is called 'one who walks' and not 'one who goes out,' for he did not depart completely, since his thought is still there. And therefore it is difficult for the author of this midrash: who permitted Jacob to leave his father and mother completely? To this he said, as if to resolve: because he went to his mate, and the verse says (Genesis 2:24), 'Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother and cling to his wife.'" "Another matter: for we find that Jacob was punished for those twenty-two years during which he did not fulfill the commandment of honoring father and mother. And one may object: did he not go with the permission of his father and mother and at their command? Why was he punished for all those days? For even if you say that he transgressed what his mother said (Genesis 27:44), 'and dwell with him a few days' — nevertheless, why was he punished for all the days? Rather, it is certainly because his father said to him, 'Arise, go to Paddan-aram' (Genesis 28:2) — he permitted him to go, but not to depart completely. For anyone who goes and intends to return, or whose thought still wanders there, is called 'one who walks' and not 'one who goes out.' But this is not what he did; rather, 'and Jacob went out' (Genesis 28:10) completely, as though he had forgotten his entire father's and mother's household. Therefore he was punished measure for measure: one of his sons, namely Joseph, did not honor him for twenty-two years, and Joseph did not remember him and forgot him, as it is written (Genesis 41:51), 'for Elohim has made me forget all my toil and all my father's house.' And Joseph knew that this was from HaShem, therefore he said, 'Elohim has made me forget.' But in his righteousness he had a desire and wish always to have his father's house as a remembrance before his eyes, but he could not, for Elohim had made him forget."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrases לְפוּם רִיהֲטָא and סָלְקָא דַעְתָּךְ are rendered as 'according to the initial reasoning' and 'you might think,' preserving the dialectical register."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:57.662531159Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}